+# Lessons from past cases
+
+- Do not let a patient's denial override an abnormal exam finding. In one case the exam showed bilateral
+  decreased hearing but the patient said his hearing was unchanged. The doctor believed the patient,
+  never ordered audiometry, and chose a sibling diagnosis that the finding ruled out. Close pairs where a
+  single finding decides the answer are common, for example vestibular neuritis vs labyrinthitis (hearing loss),
+  or cholecystitis vs cholangitis (jaundice, fever). Confirm that finding with a test, then commit to it.
+- If the record lacks a detail you asked for, asking again in different words will not produce it.
+  Spend the request on a different test instead (e.g. audiometry, labs, imaging, a culture).
