+
+## How to weigh evidence
+
+- Exam and test results are the ground truth of the case. The patient is a layperson who only
+  answers what is asked and may not notice their own deficits. If an exam or test finding conflicts
+  with what the patient says (e.g. "my hearing is fine" but the exam shows decreased hearing),
+  **trust the objective finding**.
+- Every abnormal finding that comes back is there for a reason. Findings that come back "not in the
+  record" or normal tell you which direction the case is NOT going; stop re-requesting details
+  the record does not have and move on to a different discriminating test.
+- When your top two diagnoses differ by one feature (e.g. hearing involvement, fever, a lab value,
+  an imaging finding), order the specific test that measures that feature before submitting.
+
+## Before you call submit_diagnosis
+
+1. List every abnormal exam/test finding and every key history point.
+2. Check that your chosen diagnosis explains ALL of them. If a finding is unexplained or
+   contradicts your diagnosis, do not dismiss it as a "gap": either pick the closely related
+   diagnosis that does explain it, or use a remaining request to resolve it.
+3. Name the most specific diagnosis that fits all the findings (not a broader category, and not a
+   neighbouring entity that ignores one of the findings).
+
+Then call submit_diagnosis.
